      "writtenOn": "2024-11-25T21:03:29Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Hrm I thought this was in the integration docs, but I guess not. \n\nThe dm-verity hash tree is completely untrusted -- it\u0027s only the root hash that is trusted and authenticated via the vbmeta data (so it doesn\u0027t matter if someone can rebuilt the tree, etc).\n\nThe salt is there to ensure that the hashes in the tree change even if the data blocks do not.  For instance, a partition may have 4kb blocks of all 0s which persist across releases.  Without a salt, an attacker could search for a 4kb block of data which has a hash collision with nothing constraining their search time.  The use of an unpredictable salt changed according to some schedule puts restrictions on the attacker\u0027s time window (and thus changing compute costs, etc).\n\nDoes that make sense, or am I missing something?\n\nWith that in mind, it\u0027d be nice if we could still use something unpredictable but rotate it less frequently (e.g., once per release).",
      "revId": "02001848b36a8ee1011c9daabcc9fa0f5e22f12d",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
